 # View-Dependent Appearance and Lighting
-
-
 ## Overview
-This configuration extends the standard NeRF representation by explicitly modeling the angular dependence of surface appearance and lighting, allowing the network to predict view-dependent effects such as specular reflections, anisotropic materials, and refraction/reflection in transparent objects [G]. It sets itself apart from view-independent baselines by separating or jointly encoding directionality into the radiance field, enabling accurate rendering of glossy and transparent scenes that simple density-color mappings cannot capture [G]. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [UrbanIR](16200-urbanir.md) and [REF2-NeRF](18124-ref2-nerf.md) by focusing on the general architectural conditioning of color on viewing direction, rather than specific inverse rendering or transparency modeling tasks [G].
-
